  "alt": "Two ways to change a memory. The append-only ledger inserts a new row and stamps the old one as superseded, so v1, v2, and v3 all remain and any point in time can be recovered. A destructive update overwrites the row: v2 replaces v1, and the prior state is gone.",
  "caption": "Inserting a new row and stamping valid_to on the old one gives point-in-time recovery for free.",
  "embed": "<figure class=\"np-figure\">\n  <a class=\"np-figure-zoom\" href=\"/static/images/diagrams/memory-versioning/append-vs-overwrite.svg\"><picture><source media=\"(max-width: 712px)\" srcset=\"/static/images/diagrams/memory-versioning/append-vs-overwrite.narrow.svg\" width=\"288\" height=\"1000\"><img class=\"np-has-narrow\" src=\"/static/images/diagrams/memory-versioning/append-vs-overwrite.svg\" width=\"720\" height=\"544\" alt=\"Two ways to change a memory. The append-only ledger inserts a new row and stamps the old one as superseded, so v1, v2, and v3 all remain and any point in time can be recovered. A destructive update overwrites the row: v2 replaces v1, and the prior state is gone.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></picture></a>\n  <figcaption>Inserting a new row and stamping valid_to on the old one gives point-in-time recovery for free.</figcaption>\n</figure>"
